1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 { White chooses to trade off Black's knight for a bishop. This reduces Blacks' control of the center, and alleviates the risk that White's bishop may become trapped on the queenside. Black gets the bishop pair, but at the cost of doubled pawns.
(a) 4...dxc6 is the main move. Taking back this way, though it takes a pawn away from the center, opens lines for Black's queen and queen's bishop to enter the game. Black's e5 pawn is still defended tactically for now. Nxe5 may be met with ...Qd4 forking the knight and e4 pawn. So, before White can win it they must defend the e4 pawn either 5. O-O intending Re1, or 5. Nc3. An alternative is 5.d4 attacking e5 and opening the center.
(b) 4...bxc6, the Lutikov variation leaves White with a small advantage. White can win a pawn but Black gets compensation, } 4... dxc6 (4... bxc6 5. Nxe5 Qe7 6. d4 d6 7. Nxc6 Qxe4+ 8. Qe2 Qxe2+ 9. Kxe2 { White up a pawn but having lost castling rights, the position is not cut-and-dry. More critical is to open the position and press their lead in development. }) 5. O-O { The main White move is to castle. } (5. Nxe5 Qd4 6. Nf3 Qxe4+ { If White takes the e5 pawn then Black can take back e4 pawn. } 7. Qe2 Qxe2+ 8. Kxe2) *


