1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 { 3...Nf6 is the main sideline to 3...a6. The Berlin defense is known for being solid and is used at the top level of chess as a reliable drawing weapon. The mainline continuation leads an equal endgame known as the Berlin Wall, where it is difficult for White to achieve better than a draw. Another common variation leads to a quick draw by repetition by turn 14.
White has a few options:
(a) 4. O-O allows black to take the e4 pawn. This is a trade because White can recover the material by applying pressure on the e-file.
(b) 4 d3 avoid these main Berlin defense continuations. It defends e4 so defuses the threat of 4...Nxe4 but also reinforce White's threat on e5.
(c) 4 Qe2 is a tricky sideline. It, too, defends e4 so avoid the trade of e-pawns, but also defends f2 defusing the ...Bc5 ... Qd4 ...Qxf2# threat.
(d) Nxe5 is so-called Halloween attack. It resembles the well-known Halloween gamit but worse becuase Nc3 has been replaced by Bb5, reducing White's space advantage. } 4. O-O { This move is known as lanph3re gambit.
Black has several options
(a) 4...Nxe4 taking the pawn. This is actually a trade.
(b) 4...Bc5 the Beverwijk variation is the main alternative to 4...Nxe4. Black pressures f2 and White's castled king. This is sharp because 5. Re1 to defend e4 but un-defends f2. So after 5...Ng4, White finds themselves having to move it back 6. Rf1. White has the choice to capture e5 due to a tactic that arises from the c5 and e5 squares being forkable. More commonly, White plays for c3 and d4 instead.
(c) 4...Be7 before taking the pawn shelters Black's king and gets ready to castle. This prevents any mischief around a discovered check. Now the threat of 5...Nxe4 is more severe, as while White can still recover the pawn, they would have to give up the bishop pair to do so.
(d) 4...Ng4, the Fishing pole variation, invites White to blunder by accepting a sacrificial knight but opening the h-file and exposing themselves to potential checkmate. } 4... Nxe4 (4... Bc5 5. Re1 Ng4 6. Rf1 (6. d4 { 6. d4 is blunder } 6... Nxd4 7. Nxd4 Qh4)) (4... Be7 5. c3 Nxe4 6. Re1 Nd6 7. Bxc6 dxc6 8. Nxe5 Bf5 { 4...Be7 functionally declines the gambit as it gives White time to play Re1 and defend the e4 pawn. It is typically a quieter continuation. }) (4... Ng4 5. h3 h5 6. c3 a6) 5. Re1 Nd6 6. Nxe5 Nxe5 (6... Nxb5 7. Nxc6+ { Black loses his queen. }) 7. Rxe5+ Be7 8. Bf1 O-O *


