1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 f5 { 3...f5 introduces Schliemann defense (or Jaenisch Gambit) is actually not a gambit. Despite the apparent similarity to the Latvian gambit, Black's knight on c6 changes everything.
Unlike in the Latvian, White can't capture on e5 immediately, and capturing on e5 after exchanging on c6 (4. Bxc6 dxc6 5 Nxe5) doesn't win the pawn either because ...dxc6 let Black's queen into the game and the usual Ruy Lopez rejoinder 5...Qd4 wins it straight back.
The pawn on f5 isn't free either. 4. exf5 and 4...e4 embarrasses the knight, who (again because of the knight on c6) doesn't have e5 or d4 squares to move to.
So rather than blast the Jaenisch off the board, White must act natural and ait to exploit Black's weakened kingside further down the line.
So the real option for White are defending the e4:
(a) 4 Nc3 is the Dyckhoff variation. Recapturing with the knight (4...fxe4 5. Nxe4) invites 5...d5 and leads to the hair-raising sacrifice 6 Nxe5 and a razor-sharp repudiation of the gambit.
(b) d3 is a popualr and less sharp alternative, intending to recapture on e4 with the pawn. } 4. Nc3 (4. d3 fxe4 5. dxe4 Nf6) (4. Bxc6 { White cannot take Black's e-pawn for free here. } 4... dxc6 5. Nxe5 Qd4 { The usual Ruy Lopez Queen rejoinder trick to win back a pawn. The intermezzo 6 Qh5+ does not work. } 6. Qh5+ g6 7. Nxg6 hxg6 { The rook is defended by Black's queen. } 8. Qxh8 Qxh8) (4. exf5 e4 5. Qe2 Qe7 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. Nd4 Qe5 8. Nf3 Qe7 9. Nd4 { Draw in repetition. }) 4... fxe4 5. Nxe4 d5 6. Nxe5 dxe4 7. Nxc6 Qg5 8. Qe2 Nf6 *


