1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d5 { 8...d5 is the Marshall gambit. Black is attacking e4 twice and in general threatening to overrun the center of the board. White has no better option than to accept the proffered pawn with
(a) 9 exd5
(b) 9 d4: White declines the gambit. } 9. exd5 { Black has two responses:
(a) 9...Nxd5
(b) 9...e4 is the aggressive Steiner variation, and has given Black surprising success at the amateur } 9... Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 { Black takes the opportunity to swap off White's useful central knight and instead draw the e1 rook out to a vulnerable square on the fifth rank. } 11. Rxe5 { Black's knight must be saved from the combined attack by White's bishop and rook.
When the American master Frank Marshall surprised future World Champion Jose Capablanca with the Marshall Gambit in 1918, his chosen follow-up was 11...Nf6, returning the knight to the kingside where Black's attack will be taking place. But in the century since then, trial and error has caused 11...c6 to evolve into the mainline. } 11... c6 { Black's move ...c6 nails down the d5 knight. For White to chop it off (12. Bxd5) would now leave Black with the bishop pair on an open board, and three queenside pawns that would tightly restrain White's four. White's responses are
(a) 12 d4 is a natural developing move and the main line. Skip ahead to the first major fork in the road, at 14 ... Qh3
(b) 12 d3 is a significant sideline favored by Alexey Shirov. The pawn itself will be weaker for being on d3 instead of d4, but in return White gains the possibility of quickly converting the extra pawn into a passed pawn with a later c4 push. 12 d3 scores slightly better than 12 d4 at master level and is significantly less analyzed. } 12. d4 (12. d3 Bd6 13. Re1 Bf5 14. Qf3 Qh4 15. g3 Qh3) 12... Bd6 13. Re1 Qh4 14. g3 (14. h3 Bxh3 15. gxh3 Qxh3 16. Nd2 Bh2+ 17. Kh1 Bg3+ 18. Kg1 Qh2+ 19. Kf1 Qxf2#) 14... Qh3 { We have arrived at a critical point in the Marshall Gambit. Black has a simple threat of ...Bg4, after which the White queen will have to guard against the checkmate threat with ...Bf3 and ...Qg2. This would draw the queen away from the defense of the e1-rook and the unsupported rook would then have difficulty resisting a Black takeover of the e-file starting with ...Rae8.
White has two options
(a) 15 Be3 (Mainline)
The old main line is 15 Be3, White pre-emptively blocks the e-file against Black's potential takeover. 15 Be3 makes sense in the context of White's development; the b1-knight will have to come to d2 if it hopes to influence matters on the kingside, but playing it immediately would box the c1-bishop, so get that bishop out of the way first.
But what does the bishop do on e3? Well, nothing. Black has no threats against either f2 or d4, and a few moves down the line White may well be forced to meet a Black pawn push to f5 with the blocking move f4 (to stop Black playing ...f4 herself), at which point the bishop itself will be a target of the d5-knight and a rook on e8.
(b) 15 Re4
If White wants to take decisive action against the invading Black queen, the move 15. Re4 threatening Rh4 and preventing ...Bg4 is an excellent alternative to the main line. } 15. Be3 (15. Re4 g5 16. Qf1 Qh6 17. Re1 Nf4 18. Bxf4 gxf4 19. Qd3 Kh8 20. Nd2 f5 21. Kg2 Ra7 22. Nf3 Rg7 23. Rh1 Qg6) 15... Bg4 16. Qd3 Rae8 (16... Bf3 17. Qf1 Qf5) (16... f5 17. Qf1 Qh5 18. f4 g5 19. Nd2) 17. Nd2 Re6 18. Qf1 Qh5 19. f3 Nxe3 20. Qf2 Nd5 21. fxg4 Qxg4 { Black takes back the sacrificed pawn. The position is roughly equal. } *


